  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA cold front is expected to sag down into the Great Lakes region\nWednesday into Friday. This will generally bring ascent and a\nsaturating trend to the atmospheric column. Rain chances \nincrease throughout the day on Wednesday. A few showers are \npossible in the morning (highest north of I-80) in weak ascent, \nbut highest chances work their way into the I-80 corridor and \nportions on northeast Ohio late Wednesday with models hinting at\na stronger pre-frontal trough in mid-level ascent, and better \ntiming to utilize daytime destabilization. At this moment, \nmedian SBCAPE is only a few hundred Joules, with sfc-6km shear \nat 20-25kts and DCAPE not particularly high at 400. This alludes\nto low severe potential, however PWATS pushing 1.8-2.0 inches \nallude to flood threats, which will likely only be realized in \nthe event of training.\n\nA slow moving frontal passage into Thursday will bring higher\nchancres of rain, overall. Shear remains non-dissimilar from \nthe day prior, though ensembles indicate an uptick in \ninstability (1000-1500 J/kg) and DCAPE (600-900). PWATs at this \npoint will be pushing 2.0-2.1 inches, which would be an all-time\nrecord for that calendar date. Though flow will generally be \nprogressive, with a slow moving front, we will have to monitor \nfor training potential in storms. Overall, the severe risk \nremains very low and the flood risk remains low, but increasing \nsome. Rain chances persist through Thursday night, generally \nmoving south of I-70 by Friday morning as the front sags. With \nprobabilistic guidance dominated by global model output this \nfar, we will have to wait for convective models to start being \ningested to better estimate high-end rain potential. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThere is fairly high confidence that the region will sit on the\nnortheast side of a east-central CONUS ridge into the weekend, \nwhich generally brings south-southwest surface flow and west- \nnorthwest flow aloft. This will keep more moisture, allow for \nshortwave passage potential and, generally, a pattern of \nmoisture overrunning. This favors wet conditions Saturday \nthrough Monday in showers/storms with more seasonable \ntemperatures. This confidence breaks down beyond Monday as the \npattern is more bi- modal. Either a front pushes south and \nbrings in cooler and drier conditions for next week, or it \nremains wetter, warmer, and more unsettled should the upper flow\nnot change much.",
